it might be strange to remember now but  there was a time when the summer box  office wasn't dominated by comic book  movies how far back can you trace the  origins of our current super heroic  movie landscape it just might start in  the year 2000 when x-men changed  everything  looking at the opening sequence of a  film like 1997 s Batman and Robin it's  the hero who's immediately set up as the  main character in this case it's both  Batman and Robin chicks dig the car this  is why Superman works alone everything  about it is cheesy and over-the-top and  then x-men came along and did something  completely different the film opens in a  Nazi concentration camp in 1944 with a  boy who separated from his parents makes  a desperate attempt to reopen the camps  metal gates with the superhuman powers  the boy is a young Erik Lehnsherr who  would go on to become the film's primary  antagonist magneto but we don't see him  as a villain at least not at first  we see him as a child subjected to  unimaginable horror when it is revealed  that the child has grown into the  villain it's as a sympathetic one fast  forward to the present and it's clear  that the trend of creating sympathetic  villainous hasn't gone away x-men played  a huge role in introducing the idea of a  villainous monster you could actually  care about this also blurred the line  between good and evil while Professor X  advocates for peace magneto is convinced  the only outcome is war but ultimately  they both want the same thing for mutant  kind to be raised up from oppression in  his review of the film  Roger Ebert said x-men is at least not a  manic editing frenzy for atrophied  attention spans its restrained and  introspective for a superhero epic in  fact much of x-men's  hour and 44 minute runtime is spent on  character development introducing  audiences to the main players explaining  their special abilities and delving into  their personal struggles against bigotry  and discrimination fight scenes and  action sequences are there but they're  not the main focus of the film so read  my mind I'd rather not come on you  afraid you might like it it might have  something to do with the fact that  director Bryan Singer had never read the  comics as a kid singer told the Los  Angeles Times in 2016 that it wasn't  until he started watching  the animated series later on in life  that he began to form an attachment to  the characters he explained when I  suddenly come across this universe  that's all about misfits and outcasts  who become superheroes it intrigued me I  started to figure a way to tell this  story not just to the fanbase but to a  wider audience the focus of the film  became a social message as opposed to  the big stuff audiences had come to  expect from action blockbusters magneto  wasn't the only x-men character to get  his on-screen introduction via a  relatable human back story in  Mississippi we see 17-year old Murray  accidentally put her boyfriend into a  coma after she kisses him for the first  time regardless of the continuity  between her portrayal in the original  comics and in the 2000 film Murray later  rogue wasn't introduced as a fully  fledged misfit superhero she was our  point of view character a regular person  with not so regular problems Bryan  Singer told the Los Angeles Times that  he treated the characters in x-men with  quote the same seriousness as those in  the usual suspects that legitimacy was  crucial to making a satisfying movie he  explained saying you can't just take a  bunch of characters and throw them  together and stir them up in a soup and  hope that it works from the beginning  x-men worked to set itself apart from  its comic movie predecessors via its  tone and approached the storytelling  Bryan Singer went into the movie from a  standpoint of really wanting it to be a  serious film set in a fantastic world  but he was also keenly aware of how  audiences would feel going into a  so-called comic movie in an interview  with MTV News Hugh Jackman who played  Wolverine throughout the franchise said  that singer was so dedicated to making  x-men stand out as more than just a  comic film that he wouldn't even allow  comic books onto the set thereby  effectively making source material  contraband hey where are all my comic  books oh those dreadful things I gave  those away  Jackman went on to explain he really  wanted to take calm evil characters  seriously a real three-dimensional  characters needed people who don't  understand these comics might think  they're 2-dimensional the three biggest  films of the 2000 summer box office were  headlined by Tom Cruise Russell Crowe  and George Clooney respectively at that  point in time all  three had proven themselves as leading  men x-men stars were a couple of actors  who were in their 60s a pre Gandalf Ian  McKellen and Patrick Stewart then best  known for telling Wesley Crusher to shut  up in Star Trek TNG but shut up Wesley  the two leads were supported by a mostly  unknown cast who had done some TV work  and a teen horror flick here and there  Halle Berry had done some high-profile  movie work but she wouldn't go on to win  her Oscar for monsters ball or become a  box-office force of her own for a couple  more years speaking with mtv news hugh  jackman said he went into the audition  as if it weren't a real one because he  thought Dougray Scott was already going  to play the role of Wolverine I think I  was sent up there just to kind of send a  message back to Mission Impossible to  like you better hurry out because we're  gonna start looking at other paper right  I didn't think it was a real audition  what x-men proved however is that she  didn't need a big star to sell a film to  audiences end game marked a high for  Marvel Studios and for the comic film  genre but chances are it wouldn't exist  had x-men not first paved the way in  1996 Marvel filed for bankruptcy and  sold off the cinematic rights to several  of its properties in an effort to stay  afloat 20th Century Fox picked up the  rights to the x-men and although the  movie was a success at the box office  Marvel wound up with zero to show for it  two years later the same would happen  with Sony spider-man and again Marvel  would walk away with nothing in 2005  after x-men and spider-man had proven  how successful a comic film could be if  done properly Marvel gambled everything  they had to launch their own production  studio with Iron Man assuring in the  Marvel Cinematic Universe three years  later love it or hate it x-men was built  to begin a franchise and it made major  use of a cliffhanger after the film's  storyline was wrapped up it's a common  thing now but back then films didn't  typically rely on such gimmicks the  ending of the film sees Professor X  visits magneto in his plastic cell where  magneto shares his plan to continue his  fight it's obvious this isn't the real  end of the story and that there's a  definitive plan for more and now we  watch comic films and actually expect a  tease for more I'm here to talk to you  about  Avenger initiative by the time x-men  arrived in theaters it had been over a  decade since Tim Burton's first Batman  movie had set the box office on fire in  the time since superhero movies had  hardly been shown to be a guaranteed  path to blockbuster success even  Batman's sequels had turned into a game  of diminishing returns what killed the  dinosaurs 2000s x-men proved two things  the first is that comic book adaptations  didn't have to be campy  it also proved that comic films could be  profitable longtime x-men comics writer  Chris Claremont told polygon in 2019 up  until then everybody had looked on  superhero films as losers then the x-men  rolled in with a 9 figure opening  weekend which nobody saw coming 20 years  later we are in the world of today with  Black Panther pulling in three Oscars  and into the spider-verse getting Best  Animated Film check out one of our  newest videos right he